On this, one acharya makes a poetic fancy. Go. The meaning of go is also the sense. Then, if devotees are enjoying with the Lord, if they are showing prasada related to the Lord, and if their senses go outside their control, go into the sense objects, then the Lord himself goes in order to search for them and bring them under control. So first, do not worry. Do my worship. I will bring your senses under control. I am going in order to search. This is one feeling. It is not a symbol. In truth, in order to bring the Lord under control, in order to search, one should not thrash a great deal. The Lord brings the senses under control. Because he is the Lord of the senses. The Lord is going in order to search for the calves. Lord Krishna, the all-knowing. The small and the great. Sri Krishna began to search for the calves. Where and where. The mountain. In the mountain. He began to search, going into the valley here. The gorge. In the valley of the mountain. In the mountain. The grove. Grove is said where there are thick bushes. There are vines. Into those also he is going inside. He is looking. Where did the children go. He is searching. Having become completely a child. Completely in the feeling of a child. Yes, the Lord. Lord Krishna. The Supreme Brahman. The Lord, eternity, knowledge, and bliss. The all-knowing Lord is searching. Then in the mountain, in the valley, in the grove, in the cave. Have they not fallen somewhere. From greed for the grass, have they not gone that way. Have they not gone this way. He was seeing all. Searching through. He was searching. He was looking. Then, while searching for the calves in the mountain, the valley, the grove, and the cave, how beautiful is that form of the Lord. Sometimes the memory of this thing comes to us, O Lord, why was a video camera not made at that time. When all the Lord's lilas had passed, then the video. And the morsel in the hand. The morsel is with him on the hand. The Lord took this so that he might say to the children, eat. Then he also said this, see, I am going while eating. I am going while eating. So you people eat. But still those children, one morsel. Going while holding it. In the nectar of devotion, one acharya says, the Lord says, in time I leave everything. What of much. Even the beloved. Always. The devotee who has gone, never. The Lord is as if saying, when the time comes I can leave everything. But the Lord does not leave everything. He is going, taking the rice in the hand. Then they say his further intention. The palm. In Sanskrit. Always gone into the seat. Devotees always stay entered in the Lord's heart. So the Lord indicates this, let my devotees keep dwelling in my heart. And in time I can leave everything. Even his own beloved he leaves. But he cannot leave his own devotee. So the devotee held always in the palm, he never leaves. He is searching. But he has taken the devotee along in the hand. Devotee means the rice. And devotee means the one who is mad for him. Somewhere English shows the meaning more. So when the time comes, the Lord can leave everything. But he cannot leave the devotee. Let the devotee keep dwelling in his heart. So the Lord is searching here. Then the question will be, were those calves found or not. They were not there. What happened. Then this had happened. Shukadeva Goswami, singing, says.

