Having taken those calves and those keepers of the calves elsewhere, O best of the Kurus. He placed them. He who was situated in the sky. Who formerly, having seen the liberation of Aghasura. From the Lord, he had attained supreme wonder. Having seen, from the Lord, from Sri Krishna, the liberation of Aghasura, those who formerly had attained supreme wonder. He, born of the water-born. Brahma. Brahma born of the lotus. Water is water. And he whose birth is from that. Birth from water. It is birth. Birth from water is of the lotus. Then call the lotus born of water. And he whose birth happened from that. That is, Brahma. In one way Shukadeva Goswami is speaking sharply at Brahma. Of stealing the calves and the children. He is the son of one born from water. He is born of the lotus. The dull, or the water. Because no difference is accepted between da and la. In Sanskrit a difference is not accepted between da and la. He is not pleased with Brahma. From the hearing. He will steal the calves, and he will steal the children also. There the Lord was searching for his own calves. Then here Brahma lifted the cowherd friends also and took them. Then first he tells their name. He whose birth happened from the dull, from that their birth happened. The lotus born of water. From that, the birth. In one way he is making Brahma dull. Brahma is the son born of the dull. Then he had first seen the lila of his own Lord. The liberation of Aghasura. Then he wanted to see still more glory. To see another charming glory also. Whose charming glory. Of the child, by the energy. Who by mercy, by his own yoga energy, by the energy of his own form, has manifested his own child-form in this universe. In order to see the charming glory of such a Supreme Lord, Sri Krishna, what did he do. Those calves. He took the calves of the cowherds. The other keepers of the calves also. When the Lord went to search for the calves, then here he lifted the children also and took them. To see another charming glory also. This should be kept in mind. Brahma is the greatest devotee. Then of his own Lord, of his own master, one very charming glory. O tilak of the Kuru line. Having taken the children and the calves, he placed them elsewhere. Having taken them, he hid them somewhere. Wherever he will have kept them. Having taken the children and the calves, he hid them. He who was formerly situated in the sky. Why they want to see this charming glory. That formerly, being situated in the sky, he had seen this glory of his own Lord, that he gave liberation to Aghasura. A divine light came out of Aghasura's body. And that light was absorbed in the Lord. So Brahma wants to see the charming glory. On this a poetic fancy is made, why Brahma stole the calves. Then Brahma thinks, this one is wicked. Of much sin. An enemy of the gods. Cruel. Only darkness. In a former birth, a bad birth. Low. Of the quality of darkness. He had done bad conduct through many births. Then I had decided that I will make him a share of hell. I will drop him into hell. I had made a great preparation for dropping Aghasura into hell. But, having seen, with an angry mind, toward the eternal guru. He takes those cowherds and calves. But toward the eternal guru, the guru of all, Sri Bhagavan, Brahma was seeming somewhat angry. Because that writing of his own was extended and destroyed. I think. This writing of Brahma, the account, in one way the Lord destroyed. So I accept it so, that Brahma was somewhat angry. Why did he give liberation to this unfit one. Why did he give his own abode. It seems so. So they went to see more glory.

